The fork's first commit is byte-identical to that release. The second
rewrites the module path -- the module line in go.mod and every import of the
module's own packages -- and rewords two server/server.go comments that linked
the upstream wiki by URL, so that no Go file names the old path; they now name
the wiki page. Nothing else in it changes. The later commits carry seven
patches, the first four to the client package:

  1. client.NewWithOptions, which applies imap.Reader.MaxLiteralSize, Updates
     and ErrorLog before the greeting is read and before the reader goroutine
     starts. client.New is unchanged in behaviour and now calls it with no
     options.
  2. A recover in the client's reader loop, so a panic while handling a
     malformed server response closes the connection with an error instead of
     ending the process.
  3. The response-handler lock is released by a deferred unlock, so a handler
     that panics under it (patch 2's case) does not leave the next command
     blocked in registerHandler.
  4. The continuation signal is held in a channel with room for one and sent
     without blocking, instead of by a goroutine per server "+" line. A
     command discards a signal left from before it.

  5. StatusResp.Err returns a tagged NO or BAD as an *ErrStatusResp, whose
     text is the status info as before, so a caller can tell the server
     refusing a command from a response handler failing to parse what the
     server sent.
  6. Message.Parse refuses a FETCH value without its item's shape -- a UID
     or RFC822.SIZE that is not a 32-bit number (a UID must also be
     non-zero), an INTERNALDATE that is not a date-time, a FLAGS member that
     is not a string, a body section that is not a string or NIL, a key
     with no value, a key that occurs twice -- instead of storing its zero
     value, and keeps a body section sent as a quoted string. The handler
     for unilateral responses returns an EXPUNGE or FETCH that does not
     parse, or whose sequence number is 0, instead of dropping it or
     delivering it as number 0, and the reader ends the connection on it.
  7. A command the server completed before the connection ended is returned
     as completed. execute selected between the command's result and the
     reader ending at random when both were ready, and returned errClosed
     for a command the server had completed; it now takes a waiting result
     first.

Patches 1 and 2 are tagged v1.2.1-ntk.1; patches 3 and 4 are added in
v1.2.1-ntk.2; patches 5 and 6 in v1.2.1-ntk.3; patch 7 in v1.2.1-ntk.4.
Each patch has a test in client/ntk_patch_test.go.
